    <worksheet name='Chart6-Sentiment vs. Price'>
      <layout-options>
        <title>
          <formatted-text>
            <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Benton Sans Book' fontsize='14'>Sentiment vs. Price by Room Type</run>
            <run>Æ&#10;</run>
            <run fontcolor='#898989' fontname='Benton Sans Book' fontsize='10' italic='true'>Average lines split the chart into four quadrants</run>
          </formatted-text>
        </title>
        <caption>
          <formatted-text>
            <run fontcolor='#898989' fontname='Benton Sans Book' italic='true'>The two average reference lines divide listings into four quadrants. The most valuable signal is in the upper-LEFT (low price, high sentiment) — listings that deliver above-average experience below the average price. The upper-RIGHT contains established premium listings where high prices are justified by high sentiment. The lower-RIGHT is the caution zone: overpriced relative to what guests feel. Superhost listings (orange) cluster more in the upper half across all price levels, suggesting the badge captures service quality rather than pricing power.</run>
          </formatted-text>
        </caption>
      </layout-options>
      <table>
        <view>
          <datasources>
            <datasource caption='sentiment_by_listing+ (2)' name='federated.05k49r90r4hjer13dbx9z1vmgbi1 (copy)' />
          </datasources>
