    <worksheet name='Chart9-Neighborhood Opportunity Map'>
      <layout-options>
        <title>
          <formatted-text>
            <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Benton Sans Book' fontsize='14'>San Diego Neighborhood Opportunity Map</run>
            <run>Æ&#10;</run>
            <run fontcolor='#898989' fontname='Benton Sans Book' fontsize='10' italic='true'>X = median price · Y = avg sentiment · Bubble size = listing supply</run>
          </formatted-text>
        </title>
        <caption>
          <formatted-text>
            <run fontcolor='#898989' fontname='Benton Sans Book' fontsize='10' italic='true'>The Opportunity Map suggests that San Diego&apos;s most attractive neighborhoods for short-term rental investors are not necessarily the most expensive ones. A cluster of neighborhoods — including North Hills, Mission Valley, and College Area — sits in the upper-left value zone, combining above-median guest sentiment with below-median prices and enough supply to enter the market. These value plays, rather than the premium coastal markets, may offer the strongest yield per dollar invested.</run>
          </formatted-text>
        </caption>
      </layout-options>
      <table>
        <view>
          <datasources>
            <datasource caption='neigh_opportunity' name='federated.021ktvq1khf1rd1dt626n17i2u8u' />
          </datasources>
